      "math_academy_id": "q-339131",
      "knowledge_point_id": "7f458c61-bc51-5653-9ecb-1e96eb5c335e",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Limit of an Inverse Trigonometric Function at a Point",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(2\\sqrt{3}x+6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x))=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x):$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/85a182aabfe64c27eaafde94e3bdbede9409b02cfd87395cb2fc5543b09c4e05.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in [-1,1].$   \n\nNow, substituting $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ directly into the limit, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\sqrt{3}/2}(2\\sqrt{3}x+6\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)) & =2\\sqrt{3}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})+6\\cdot \\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =3+6\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =3+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
