      "knowledge_point_id": "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses",
      "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x))=$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x):$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/ca5e8035505f20619b6707d6d1f2d0b7b2f125a004a0dbd9d448840b0e4ffd99.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)$ is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
