      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1))=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13996467/assets/f5cef3bda76e80edfa467a90823c9b6ad6aca1f8a5e8e3e06dc585258e975e0a.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty ,$ the graph tends to $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
